<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    Climate zones (RimWorld: BiomeDefs). WorldGenStep_Biomes gives each tile the biome whose workerClass
    scores it highest; every land worker first checks Tile.WaterCovered (so land and water biomes never
    compete), and every water worker checks the opposite. Density/disease/movement numbers below are this
    port's own RimWorld-like approximations, not RimWorld's exact decompiled values.

    terrainsByFertility (RimWorld: BiomeDef.terrainsByFertility, read by GenStep_Terrain) is in RimWorld's
    raw-noise fertility units, about -1..1; GenStep_Terrain maps this port's 0..1 grid back to them. What is
    recalled from RimWorld and what is not:
      - The forest and wetland biomes' Soil below 0.87, SoilRich above it: recalled as RimWorld's
        TemperateForest table, and used for every biome whose own table was not recalled differently. On
        this port's fertility grid a cell almost never reaches 0.87, so, as in RimWorld, rich soil comes
        from the patch makers below.
      - Ice everywhere on SeaIce and IceSheet, and Sand everywhere on ExtremeDesert: recalled in shape.
      - Tundra, AridShrubland and Desert bands: this port's own, ordered as RimWorld's are (barest ground at
        the lowest fertility), with the cut points chosen, not sourced. Pinned by MapGen tests as shares of
        a map, not as these literals.
    Ocean and Lake carry none: no settlement map is generated on them.

    terrainPatchMakers (RimWorld: BiomeDef.terrainPatchMakers / TerrainPatchMaker, read by GenStep_Terrain
    before the fertility bands, first match wins). Patch noise is sampled at cell coordinates, so
    perlinFrequency is per cell; minFertility/maxFertility are in the same raw units as the bands. What is
    recalled and what is not:
      - The mechanism and the class defaults (frequency 0.01, lacunarity 2, persistence 0.5, 6 octaves,
        fertility window -999..999): recalled from RimWorld's TerrainPatchMaker.
      - perlinFrequency 0.027 on the forest makers: recalled, low confidence.
      - Everything else is this port's own. The layers are shaped as RimWorld's forests and wetlands read
        in play (rich-soil patches on the fertile half of a forest; small marshy hollows with mud at their
        heart; wetlands of marshy soil, marsh and standing shallow water), and the cut points were chosen
        to give, on 200x200 maps: forest rich soil about 4-7% (the share the old fertility ladder gave
        before terrainsByFertility), forest hollows about 1-4%, wetland marsh plus water about 15-35%. The
        three forests share one table, the three wetlands another, because no biome's own was recalled.
        Pinned by MapGen tests as shares of a map, not as these literals.
      - Tundra, Desert and ExtremeDesert carry none; the ice biomes neither. AridShrubland's small rich
        soil patches on its fertile ground are this port's own.
    Tile swampiness is not read by map generation: it picks the wetland biome, whose makers lay the marsh.
  -->

